From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: do not report "Not affected" on behalf of a late CPU that is

arm64/0001 lets an A72 join late carrying a LOCAL_CPU_ERRATUM the system does
not have, without enabling it. That is a deliberate trade and its cost is
recorded: those cores run unmitigated, exactly as `nospectre_v2` would leave
them.

What was not noticed is that the machine then says otherwise. Measured on build
#101, one boot, two facts:

  $ dmesg | grep permitting
  CPU features: CPU8: permitting capability 58 (Spectre-v2) on a late CPU
                without enabling it

  $ cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/vulnerabilities/spectre_v2
  Not affected
  $ cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/vulnerabilities/spec_store_bypass
  Not affected

Both files are computed from state finalised over the boot CPUs, and on this
SoC those are Cortex-A53s -- which genuinely are unaffected by Spectre-v2 and
v4. The two Cortex-A72s that arrive at ~240 s are affected, are unmitigated,
and cannot change the verdict. So anything reading those files to decide
whether hardening is needed -- a container runtime, a hardening audit, a
person -- is told the machine is safe while a fifth of its cores are not.

Running unmitigated is a trade this port makes on purpose. Concealing it is not
part of that trade.

verify_local_cpu_caps() now records the fact in a flag, and the two arm64
reporters consult it before their normal switch. Measured on #102, same boot:

  cpu8/9 offline : spectre_v2 = "Not affected"        (true: A53s only)
  cpu8/9 online  : spectre_v2 = "Vulnerable: late CPU permitted without
                                 mitigation"
                   spec_store_bypass = same

Note the flag is only set when a capability is actually permitted this way, so
a kernel that never onlines a late CPU -- or any other arm64 board using this
tree -- reports exactly what it did before.

This does not fix #60; the cores are still unmitigated and the two ways to
change that are still the two large projects that issue names. It removes the
part of #60 nobody had noticed, which is that you could not tell.

--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature.c
@@ -123,6 +123,14 @@
 static bool __read_mostly allow_mismatched_32bit_el0;
 
 /*
+ * Set when a late CPU was allowed to carry a LOCAL_CPU_ERRATUM the system does
+ * not have, without enabling it. Read by the arm64 vulnerabilities sysfs
+ * reporters so they do not claim "Not affected" on behalf of cores that are.
+ * Never cleared: such a CPU stays unmitigated for the life of the boot.
+ */
+bool __read_mostly arm64_late_cpu_unmitigated;
+
+/*
  * Static branch enabled only if allow_mismatched_32bit_el0 is set and we have
  * seen at least one CPU capable of 32-bit EL0.
  */
@@ -3171,6 +3179,30 @@
 				pr_info_once("CPU%d: permitting capability %d (%s) on a late CPU without enabling it\n",
 					     smp_processor_id(), caps->capability,
 					     caps->desc ? caps->desc : "unnamed");
+
+				/*
+				 * And say so where userspace can see it.
+				 *
+				 * Without this the machine tells a flat lie.
+				 * the files under
+				 * /sys/devices/system/cpu/vulnerabilities are
+				 * computed from the boot CPUs, and on this SoC
+				 * those are Cortex-A53s, which genuinely are
+				 * not affected by Spectre-v2 or v4. So with two
+				 * unmitigated Cortex-A72s online, spectre_v2
+				 * and spec_store_bypass both read
+				 * "Not affected" -- measured on build #101, in
+				 * the same boot whose dmesg carries "CPU8:
+				 * permitting capability 58 (Spectre-v2) on a
+				 * late CPU without enabling it".
+				 *
+				 * Anything reading those files to decide
+				 * whether hardening is needed is told the
+				 * machine is safe. Running unmitigated is a
+				 * trade this port makes deliberately (#60);
+				 * concealing it is not part of that trade.
+				 */
+				arm64_late_cpu_unmitigated = true;
 			}
 		}
 	}
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/proton-pack.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/proton-pack.c
@@ -121,6 +121,19 @@
 #endif
 }
 
+/*
+ * Set by verify_local_cpu_caps() when a late CPU was permitted a
+ * LOCAL_CPU_ERRATUM the system does not have, without enabling it.
+ *
+ * On MT6797 the boot CPUs are Cortex-A53s, which are genuinely unaffected by
+ * Spectre-v2 and v4, and the two Cortex-A72s arrive late and unmitigated. The
+ * state variables below are finalised from the A53s, so without this check
+ * these files report "Not affected" for a machine two of whose ten cores are.
+ * Reporting the mitigation this port chose not to apply is one thing;
+ * reporting the vulnerability away is another.
+ */
+extern bool arm64_late_cpu_unmitigated;
+
 ssize_t cpu_show_spectre_v2(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr,
 			    char *buf)
 {
@@ -128,6 +141,10 @@
 	const char *bhb_str = get_bhb_affected_string(bhb_state);
 	const char *v2_str = "Branch predictor hardening";
 
+	if (arm64_late_cpu_unmitigated)
+		return sprintf(buf,
+			       "Vulnerable: late CPU permitted without mitigation\n");
+
 	switch (spectre_v2_state) {
 	case SPECTRE_UNAFFECTED:
 		if (bhb_state == SPECTRE_UNAFFECTED)
@@ -446,6 +463,10 @@
 ssize_t cpu_show_spec_store_bypass(struct device *dev,
 				   struct device_attribute *attr, char *buf)
 {
+	if (arm64_late_cpu_unmitigated)
+		return sprintf(buf,
+			       "Vulnerable: late CPU permitted without mitigation\n");
+
 	switch (spectre_v4_state) {
 	case SPECTRE_UNAFFECTED:
 		return sprintf(buf, "Not affected\n");
